WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.593 --> 00:00:03.800
One of the world's biggest travel platforms once had to sell cereal

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:05.519
just to keep its startup alive.

3
00:00:05.861 --> 00:00:09.775
Back in 2007, Brian Chesky and Joe Gebbia had a problem:

4
00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:11.677
they couldn't easily afford their rent.

5
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:13.626
During a busy design conference,

6
00:00:13.781 --> 00:00:16.691
they turned their apartment into a tiny accommodation business,

7
00:00:16.869 --> 00:00:18.645
using air mattresses for guests.

8
00:00:18.973 --> 00:00:22.499
But turning that idea into a business was harder than they imagined.

9
00:00:22.664 --> 00:00:24.920
Bookings were limited, money was running out,

10
00:00:25.064 --> 00:00:27.616
and the founders were struggling to keep the company going.

11
00:00:27.949 --> 00:00:30.622
Then, during the 2008 US election,

12
00:00:30.928 --> 00:00:34.631
they created something unusual: Obama O's and Cap'n McCain's.

13
00:00:34.848 --> 00:00:36.904
They designed collectible cereal boxes,

14
00:00:37.188 --> 00:00:40.476
assembled them by hand, and sold them for forty dollars each.

15
00:00:40.794 --> 00:00:44.216
The unusual experiment brought in around thirty thousand dollars.

16
00:00:44.436 --> 00:00:46.125
It wasn't a scalable business model,

17
00:00:46.291 --> 00:00:48.932
but it gave Airbnb money to keep going and a story

18
00:00:49.012 --> 00:00:51.519
that would eventually attract the attention of investors.

19
00:00:51.849 --> 00:00:54.623
That persistence helped them get into Y Combinator.

20
00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:57.071
But they still had to solve the real problem.

21
00:00:57.288 --> 00:00:58.565
They travelled to New York,

22
00:00:58.728 --> 00:01:02.103
met hosts, and personally improved their property photographs.

23
00:01:02.328 --> 00:01:05.564
They focused on making the marketplace better for real people.

24
00:01:05.905 --> 00:01:08.528
Airbnb became a global travel platform.

25
00:01:08.764 --> 00:01:12.301
But the early lesson was already clear: when the obvious solution fails,

26
00:01:12.544 --> 00:01:17.050
creativity, persistence, and understanding your customers can open another door.
